  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Calibrated Instrument",
    "overview": "Every surface is a measuring instrument laid on cool white: grey-white grounds, near-black ink, hairline rules, and one magenta that marks the answer or the next action. Colour is never decoration. Fire colours appear only where there is fire data, the two sensor colours only where a sensor is being named, and magenta only where the reader should look or click. Charts are drawn in SVG from the data itself, with annotations written onto the plot, so the evidence carries the page rather than imagery or illustration.\n\nThe system runs at two densities that share one palette, one typeface and one set of colour roles. The tool surfaces (the calendar and the globe) are compact: 16px body, 10px-radius controls, 14px cards with a soft ambient shadow over a grey map ground. The editorial surface (the landing page) is generous: 18px body, Figtree 900 display, pill controls, and 24-28px rounded panels on white. A new surface picks one density and stays in it.\n\nThe look is deliberately scientific, not tactical: the earlier dark \"game HUD\" versions were rejected, and the light world is the incumbent.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Cool white and grey-white grounds, near-black ink, hairline dividers.",
      "One magenta for answer and action; tints of it for current state and selection.",
      "Fire ramp, MODIS purple and VIIRS teal are data encodings, never UI chrome.",
      "Figtree only, heavy weights for headlines, tabular numerals for figures.",
      "Charts are hand-drawn SVG built from the data, labelled in place.",
      "Two densities (tool and editorial) on one token set."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Voice Rule",
        "body": "Magenta means \"answer\" or \"act\". If it is on screen, it is either the conclusion of a chart, a link, a focus ring, or the primary button. It never fills a decorative shape or a section background.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fenced Data Rule",
        "body": "Fire ramp colours encode fire; MODIS violet and VIIRS teal encode sensors. None of them may style a button, a heading, or a background that is not data. A legend must exist wherever they appear.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Labelled Truth Rule",
        "body": "Data provenance is shown with a status badge: green for real data, amber for simulated. Colour is paired with a text label, never alone.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Weight Ladder Rule",
        "body": "Figtree 900 is reserved for editorial display, headlines and the chart answer; tool surfaces top out at 800. Body never goes below 400.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figure Rule",
        "body": "Numbers that are compared (counts, factors, years) use `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ground First Rule",
        "body": "Separate surfaces by ground colour before reaching for a shadow; when the ground already contrasts, drop the shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep magenta to the answer and the action: one primary button per view, the conclusive series, links, focus.",
